    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>Is an under desk elliptical worth it?</strong> For the right person, yes. If you sit at a desk for several hours a day and you will actually pedal on most workdays, a seated elliptical is one of the cheapest and easiest ways to turn idle sitting time into light activity. If you want an intense workout, have very little room under your desk, or already move a lot during the day, it is probably not worth the money.</p>
<p>The value of an under desk elliptical depends almost entirely on use. A machine that runs for two hours a day is a bargain; one that sits in a closet is a waste. This guide helps you judge which camp you will be in, shows how to think about cost per use, and lists the alternatives if it is not the right fit.</p>

<h2>What You Actually Get From an Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
<p>Before deciding whether an under desk elliptical is worth it for you, be clear on what it does and does not do.</p>
<h3>Realistic benefits</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>More daily movement with no extra time.</strong> You pedal while you work, so it does not compete with your schedule.</li>
<li><strong>A modest calorie burn.</strong> Light to moderate seated pedaling is about 2.0 to 2.8 METs on the Compendium of Physical Activities, versus about 1.3 for sitting. For most people that is roughly 100 to 200 calories an hour.</li>
<li><strong>Breaking up long sitting.</strong> Frequent light movement through the day is the idea behind NEAT (non-exercise activity thermogenesis) research associated with the Mayo Clinic.</li>
<li><strong>Gentle, low-impact motion.</strong> It may help with stiff legs and restlessness, and it suits people who find walking or standing hard.</li>
<li><strong>Focus and energy.</strong> Many owners report that light pedaling helps them stay alert during long calls or the afternoon slump.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Realistic limits</h3>
<ul>
<li>It is not a replacement for cardio or strength training.</li>
<li>It will not cause weight loss without changes to diet.</li>
<li>It gives your upper body and core almost no work.</li>
<li>Wrist trackers often miss it, so it may not count as steps.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a deeper look at results, read <a href="/does-an-under-desk-elliptical-work/">does an under desk elliptical work</a> and our guide to <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-benefits/">under desk elliptical benefits</a>.</p>

<h2>Who an Under Desk Elliptical Is Worth It For</h2>
<p>You are likely to get good value if several of these describe you:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>You sit for four or more hours a day</strong>, especially at home where nobody sees you pedaling.</li>
<li><strong>You spend a lot of time on calls, video or reading</strong>, which are the easiest tasks to pedal through.</li>
<li><strong>You struggle to fit in exercise</strong> and want something that adds movement without carving out time.</li>
<li><strong>You are older, recovering from an injury or have joint pain</strong> and want gentle, seated movement. Our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> covers stable, easy models. Check with your doctor first if you have a health condition.</li>
<li><strong>You get restless or sluggish</strong> sitting still for long periods.</li>
<li><strong>You have a regular desk</strong> and no plans to buy a standing desk.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Who Should Skip It</h2>
<p>An under desk elliptical is probably not worth it if:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>You want a real workout.</strong> Resistance on most seated models tops out at moderate effort. A full-size elliptical, bike or brisk walk is better.</li>
<li><strong>Your desk is low or has a keyboard tray.</strong> If your knees cannot clear the desk, you will not use it. Measure first with our <a href="/tools/desk-clearance-checker/">desk clearance checker</a>.</li>
<li><strong>You already stand or walk a lot</strong> at work, so the extra movement adds little.</li>
<li><strong>You want to stand while you move.</strong> Seated models are not built for that. Read <a href="/can-you-stand-on-an-under-desk-elliptical/">can you stand on an under desk elliptical</a>.</li>
<li><strong>You share a quiet office</strong> and are considering a cheap model that may squeak.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cost Per Use: How to Judge the Value</h2>
<p>The fairest way to decide if an under desk elliptical is worth it is to think in cost per session rather than sticker price. Check the current price of the model you like, then divide it by how often you expect to use it.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>How often you use it</th><th>Sessions in a year</th><th>Cost per session</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Every workday</td><td>about 250</td><td>Price ÷ 250</td></tr>
<tr><td>Three days a week</td><td>about 150</td><td>Price ÷ 150</td></tr>
<tr><td>Once a week</td><td>about 50</td><td>Price ÷ 50</td></tr>
<tr><td>First two weeks only</td><td>about 10</td><td>Price ÷ 10</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Used most workdays for a year, even a premium seated elliptical works out to a small amount per session, far less than most gym visits or fitness classes, and it keeps going in year two and three. Used for two weeks and abandoned, even a budget model is poor value.</p>
<p>That is why we usually suggest paying a little more for a quiet, smooth model. A machine that is pleasant to use is a machine you keep using. Squeaks, sliding and rough motion are the main reasons owners give up.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Buy from a seller with a clear return window, set it up properly on day one and use it daily for two weeks. If you are not using it by then, return it.</p></div>

<h2>How to Make Sure It's Worth It</h2>
<p>Most of the value comes from habits, not the machine. These steps raise your odds:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Set it up properly.</strong> A non-slip mat, a chair that does not roll (or [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]]), the right distance and low starting resistance. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-under-desk-elliptical/">how to use an under desk elliptical</a> covers each step.</li>
<li><strong>Leave it under the desk.</strong> If you have to fetch it from a closet, you will not use it.</li>
<li><strong>Link it to triggers.</strong> Pedal on every call, or every time you open your inbox.</li>
<li><strong>Use short blocks.</strong> Ten to twenty minutes several times a day beats one long session. A <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> helps.</li>
<li><strong>Track your minutes.</strong> Log sessions in your fitness app so you can see the weekly total grow.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Find Out If One Fits You</h2>
<p>If you are on the fence, answer a few quick questions about your desk, goals and noise needs. The quiz will tell you whether an under desk elliptical makes sense for you and which type to look at.</p>
